<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masyaf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Masyaf is a town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, on the eastern slopes of the coastal mountains west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>, dominated by a medieval castle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismaili-state">Nizari Ismailis</a> acquired the castle in 1140–1141, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-sinan">Rashid al-Din Sinan</a> it was the headquarters of their Syrian branch, the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>' of crusader sources; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> besieged it without success in 1176. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> around 1270, and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> community still lives in the area.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masyaf.jpg" alt="Masyaf" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amjad helo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masyaf_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Masyaf lies on the foothills of the eastern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-coastal-mountain-range">Jabal Ansariyah</a> coastal range, just west of the al-Ghab Plain, at an elevation of about 485 metres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> is 45 kilometres to the east and Baniyas 54 kilometres to the west. The surrounding land is marked by gardens, orchards and fields of wheat and barley. The name comes from the Assyrian Manṣuate, which one scholar takes to mean a raised platform, a description of the promontory on which the fortress stands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Masyaf is the most probable site of the Aramean city of Mansuate of the 8th century BCE, later the centre of an Assyrian province of that name, and probably of the Marsyas of Roman and Byzantine writers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> chroniclers first mention the fortress in 1099. In the autumn of 999 the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> destroyed its fortifications, and in 1127 the Mirdasids sold it to the Banu Munqidh of Shaizar.</p><p>In 1140 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismaili-state">Nizari Ismailis</a> captured it by ambushing and killing its defender, a Banu Munqidh mamluk named Sunqur. Persecuted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banias">Banias</a>, they had chosen to settle in the fortresses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-coastal-mountain-range">Jabal Ansariyah</a>, and with al-Kahf, Khawabi, al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadmus">Qadmus</a> and al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusafa">Rusafa</a>, Masyaf let them keep an autonomous territory among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> and local Muslim dynasties.</p><p>Rashid ad-Din Sinan's men made two unsuccessful attempts on the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who in 1176 marched against Masyaf but withdrew within days to face <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> attacks in the Beqaa; a truce was mediated by his uncle Shihab al-Din Mahmud al-Harimi. In 1249 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> leader Taj al-Din Abu'l Futuh built a wall around the town. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> briefly occupied Masyaf in 1260 and withdrew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Battle of Ain Jalut</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan Baibars demanded tribute in 1262, and in 1270 Masyaf was fully incorporated into the sultanate, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismailis</a> continued to live there. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1516–17. In 1808 the Raslan clan captured the fortress and killed about 300 Ismailis, and Ismaili control was restored in 1810 after an Ottoman force recaptured it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Masyaf had a population of 22,508 at the 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and is the administrative centre of the Masyaf District. It is a religiously mixed city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismailis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alawites">Alawites</a>, with a Christian minority. The fortress was largely abandoned by its residents after the Allied capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1917 and is now a national monument under the Syrian Directorate of Antiquities. Within the walls stands a mosque dating from the 12th century, which local residents associate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Masyaf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1132 they acquired castles in the Jabal Bahra, the coastal mountains of Syria, including Qadmus, Kahf and in 1140-1141 <b>Masyaf</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/nizari-ismailis-of-syria">Nizari Ismailis of Syria</a></li></ul></section>
